# The Wayfinder commit redline is built and tested against.
#
# Wayfinder is consumed read-only (ADR-0001) and never committed here
# (validate.sh check #6): CI and local dev *materialise* it into vendor/wayfinder
# via scripts/vendor-wayfinder.sh. This file is what makes that materialisation
# reproducible — without it CI tracked a moving `main`, so an upstream commit
# could turn redline red with nothing changed on our side, and no SHA to bisect.
#
# Why a pin file and not a git submodule: a submodule checkout puts *all* of
# Wayfinder's packages under vendor/wayfinder/packages/*, which our pnpm
# workspace glob would absorb — dragging @huggingface/transformers, the
# OpenTelemetry SDK, minio, docx and pdf-parse into redline's install. The
# vendoring script deliberately copies only the one package we consume
# (WAYFINDER_PACKAGES, default: domain). See ADR-0012.
#
# Why johntooth/wayfinder and not rbrasier/wayfinder: redline builds and runs
# against the fork, never against rbrasier directly — the same repo the
# services/wayfinder submodule mounts (.gitmodules). rbrasier is upstream of the
# fork and runs drastically ahead of it, so naming it here would let a `ref`
# bump vendor a tree the fork has never carried. CI reads this field literally
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml checks out `repo` @ `ref`), so it is a live source,
# not a label.
#
# The ref below is a commit on the fork's `main` that predates PR #9, so the
# vendored tree is still un-forked Wayfinder. Do not read that as "main is the
# clean mirror" — it no longer is. PR #9 merged `redline-integration` into main
# (main's tip now carries redline's own `evaluation:review` permission), which
# breaches the invariant validate.sh #15b asserts: redline changes must stay off
# main so upstreaming remains a clean diff. That breach is unresolved; until it
# is, choose a bump ref deliberately rather than assuming any main commit is
# upstream-clean.
#
# To bump: change ref below, re-run scripts/vendor-wayfinder.sh, then
# `pnpm install && ./validate.sh`. The drift check
# (packages/redline-adapters/src/wayfinder/wayfinder-contract.test.ts) is what
# tells you whether the new commit still satisfies the contract redline reuses.

repo=johntooth/wayfinder
ref=60767f41ec3ee2a793897dbee6a31cc62e967fef
